    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">Do insurance adjusters make good money? For a career you can start in weeks, without a college degree, the answer is yes. The typical adjuster earns a solid middle-class salary with benefits, and experienced specialists and catastrophe adjusters can earn well into six figures. But the money comes with real trade-offs, and it is not the same for everyone. Here is the honest picture.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Yes. The BLS median for claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators was $76,790 a year in May 2024. Entry-level adjusters typically start at $45,000 to $60,000, senior and large loss adjusters commonly earn $85,000 to $120,000 or more, and experienced CAT adjusters can earn far more in big storm years.</div>

<h2 id="do-insurance-adjusters-make-good-money-the-numbers">Do insurance adjusters make good money? The numbers</h2>
<p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics counted 365,300 jobs in this occupation group in 2024, with a median pay of $76,790 a year, or $36.92 an hour. That median is well above the median for all US workers, and it is achievable without a four-year degree.</p>
<p>Pay varies by role. These are typical industry ranges for staff (company) adjusters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Trainee / entry-level:</strong> $45,000 to $60,000</li>
<li><strong>Auto / material damage:</strong> $50,000 to $72,000</li>
<li><strong>Desk property:</strong> $55,000 to $75,000</li>
<li><strong>Field property:</strong> $62,000 to $90,000, plus vehicle and benefits worth $8,000 to $15,000</li>
<li><strong>Bodily injury / casualty:</strong> $60,000 to $88,000</li>
<li><strong>Senior / complex / large loss:</strong> $85,000 to $120,000+</li>
</ul>
<p>Independent adjusters paid per claim have a wider range: slow years of $30,000 to $60,000, average active years of $70,000 to $120,000, and occasional storm years above $150,000 for experienced CAT adjusters. For a deeper breakdown, see <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/how-much-do-insurance-adjusters-make/">how much insurance adjusters make</a>.</p>

<h2 id="who-earns-the-most">Who earns the most as an adjuster</h2>
<p>The top earners share a few traits: specialized skills, a willingness to travel or take on complexity, and years of experience.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Experienced CAT adjusters.</strong> Deployments after hurricanes, hail and wildfires run two weeks to several months and commonly pay $15,000 to $60,000 or more each. Model a deployment with the <a href="/cat-deployment-calculator/">CAT Deployment Earnings Calculator</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Large loss and commercial adjusters.</strong> Complex files with big dollar values command senior salaries and higher daily rates.</li>
<li><strong>Established public adjusters.</strong> PAs earn a contingency fee, typically 10% to 20% of the settlement, and those with steady commercial work can earn $150,000 or more.</li>
<li><strong>Senior staff and supervisors.</strong> Carrier leaders with AIC or CPCU designations reach the top of staff pay bands.</li>
</ol>

<p>What separates high earners from average ones is rarely luck. It is usually speed and accuracy with estimating software, a record of clean files that earns repeat deployments, and multiple state licenses so you can go wherever claims are. Adjusters who treat the job as a craft, keep learning and build relationships with IA firms and carrier managers tend to climb fastest.</p>

<h2 id="is-adjusting-good-money-in-texas">Is adjusting good money in Texas?</h2>
<p>Texas is one of the strongest states to answer yes. It is among the largest adjuster markets in the country, with spring hail and tornadoes, Gulf Coast hurricanes, winter freezes like the February 2021 storm, and wildfire risk out west. Staff pay runs roughly in line with the national median, and Texas has no state income tax, so more of each paycheck stays with you.</p>

