<p>These are our estimates, not official figures; the BLS national median of $76,790 is the benchmark. Independent adjusters based in Texas also benefit from storms within driving distance, which cuts travel and lodging costs. The Texas all-lines license is the most popular designated home state license, so getting licensed here also opens doors in other states. Learn more in our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">insurance adjuster salary Texas</a> guide, the <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/texas-adjuster-license-salary/">Texas adjuster license salary</a> guide, or the <a href="/salary-by-state/#TX">Texas entry on our salary by state page</a>.</p>

<h2 id="pros-and-cons-of-adjusting">Pros and cons of the money and the work</h2>
<h3>Pros</h3>
<ul>
<li>Above-median pay without a degree requirement.</li>
<li>Paid training and benefits at most carriers.</li>
<li>Field roles often include a company car, gas card and phone.</li>
<li>Huge upside for independent and CAT adjusters.</li>
<li>Skills are portable across states, carriers and lines of business.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cons</h3>
<ul>
<li>Heavy caseloads and constant deadlines.</li>
<li>You deliver bad news, and some customers are upset or hostile.</li>
<li>CAT work means 7-day weeks, 12-hour days and weeks away from home.</li>
<li>Independent income is irregular, with no benefits and out-of-pocket expenses.</li>
<li>BLS projects a 5% employment decline from 2024 to 2034 as simple claims are automated, though thousands of openings still appear each year.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="do-insurance-adjusters-get-bonuses-for-denying-claims">Do insurance adjusters get bonuses for denying claims?</h2>
<p>This is one of the most common questions about the job, and it deserves a careful answer. At reputable carriers, <strong>adjusters are not paid bonuses for denying claims</strong>. Every state has unfair claim settlement practices laws that prohibit misrepresenting coverage, failing to investigate promptly, or refusing to pay valid claims. An adjuster who denied valid claims to earn a bonus would be exposing the carrier to regulatory penalties and bad faith lawsuits.</p>
<p>That said, adjusters do work under performance metrics. Common ones include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cycle time:</strong> how quickly claims are resolved.</li>
<li><strong>File quality:</strong> documentation and adherence to guidelines.</li>
<li><strong>Customer satisfaction</strong> scores.</li>
<li><strong>Accuracy, or "leakage":</strong> paying more or less than the policy actually owes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some carriers tie annual bonuses to team or company results, and critics argue that cost-focused metrics can create pressure to pay less. The healthier way to see leakage is as accuracy in both directions: underpaying a valid claim is also an error. If you are a policyholder and believe a claim was handled in bad faith, you can contact your state insurance department, and you may consider a licensed public adjuster. Our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/how-do-insurance-adjusters-get-paid/">how insurance adjusters get paid</a> guide explains compensation structures in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="adjuster-vs-four-year-college">4-year college vs becoming an adjuster</h2>
<p>One reason adjusting appeals to career changers is how quickly you can start earning. Here is how the path compares with a traditional four-year degree.</p>

